{
  "openapi": "3.1.0",
  "info": {
    "title": "Telephone API",
    "description": "Telephone is an MCP server that acts as a switchboard, so two AI agents (two Codex CLI sessions, or ChatGPT and Codex) can hold a two-way conversation with no human relaying messages.",
    "version": "0.1.1"
  },
  "paths": {
    "/health": {
      "get": {
        "tags": [
          "health"
        ],
        "summary": "Liveness probe (public)",
        "description": "Public liveness signal: this process is up and serving.\n\nRuns **no dependency probes**.  Liveness and readiness are different\nquestions and this endpoint answers only the first, for three reasons:\n\n- ``db/engine.py`` builds the engine with ``pool_pre_ping=True`` and no\n  connect timeout, so a database host that stops answering SYN blocks the\n  probe on OS TCP retries (~130s on Linux).  The Dockerfile healthcheck is\n  ``--timeout=5s --retries=3``, so probing here would turn a slow\n  dependency into a container restart loop.\n- This is a sync ``def``, so it runs on the anyio threadpool.  A probe that\n  blocks lets unauthenticated callers park every thread in that pool and\n  stall every other sync route in the app.  ``db/engine.py`` now bounds the\n  connect at ``CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS``, but a bounded probe on an\n  anonymous endpoint is still a lever an attacker can pull for free.\n- A per-component rollup readable by anonymous callers is a disclosure\n  oracle in its own right (\"one of their backends is erroring right now\").\n\nNothing consumes the rollup: Railway ``healthcheckPath``, Render\n``healthCheckPath`` and the Dockerfile ``HEALTHCHECK`` all assert on the\nstatus code alone.  Readiness lives on ``/health/detail``.",
        "operationId": "health_check_health_get",
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "description": "Successful Response",
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {}
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "/health/detail": {
      "get": {
        "tags": [
          "health"
        ],
        "summary": "Detailed health (authenticated)",
        "description": "Full health payload - build identity plus per-component status.\n\nAuthenticated-only: the git commit identifies the exact deployed revision\nof an open-source codebase, which is exactly the disclosure ASVS V14.3.3\nwarns about.\n\nThis is a sync ``def`` and it does probe, so the threadpool argument in\n``health_check`` applies here too - authentication changes who can pull the\nlever, not that it exists.  What bounds it is ``db/engine.py``'s\n``CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS``: the Redis client already carries its own\nsocket timeouts and the Stripe probe makes no network call, so with the\ndatabase connect bounded, no probe here can hold a worker indefinitely.",
        "operationId": "health_detail_health_detail_get",
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "description": "Successful Response",
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {}
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json": {
      "get": {
        "tags": [
          "well-known"
        ],
        "summary": "Mcp Server Card",
        "description": "SEP-2127 Server Card - pre-connect registry/client discovery.\n\nLets agents preview the server before opening a transport: identity\n(``name``/``title``/``description``/``version``/``icons``), where to connect\n(``serverUrl`` + ``remotes``), and the tool surface (``tools[]``).\n\nNo ``$schema`` is emitted: the draft SEP-2127 server-card schema is not yet\npublished (the URL 404s), so advertising it would only break validators.",
        "operationId": "mcp_server_card__well_known_mcp_server_card_json_get",
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "description": "Successful Response",
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {}
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
